12_23_23_28_01	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Chess player. Chess is a 2-player game playing on a 8x8 board, and the goal is to checkmate your opponent\'s king.\n        ## Pieces & Movement:\n\n        * King (K): Moves one square in any direction.\n\n        * Queen (Q): Moves any distance in any straight or diagonal line.\n\n        * Rook (R): Moves any distance horizontally or vertically.\n\n        * Bishop (B): Moves any distance diagonally.\n\n        * Knight (N): Moves in "L" shape: 2 squares one way + 1 square perpendicular.\n\n        * Pawn (P):\n\n            * Forward 1 square (never backward)\n\n            * First move: optionally 2 squares forward\n\n            * Captures diagonally forward\n\n            * Promotion: Reaches last rank → any piece (except king)\n        \n        ## Special rules: \n        * Castling: King moves 2 squares toward rook, rook jumps over. Conditions:\n\n            * Neither piece has moved\n\n            * No pieces between\n\n            * King not in check\n\n            * King doesn\'t pass through attacked squares\n\n        * En Passant: Pawn that moved 2 squares can be captured by adjacent enemy pawn as if it moved 1 square.\n\n        * Check: King under attack must escape immediately.\n\n        * Checkmate: King in check with no legal moves → game ends.\n\n        * Stalemate: King not in check but has no legal moves → draw.\n\n        ## Board and Chess piece Representation:\n        * Board is represented as [a-h][1-8]\n        * Origin: bottom-left corner (a1) \n        * White side: Bottom (ranks 1-2)\n        * Black side: Top (ranks 7-8)\n        * X-axis: Files `a` to `h` (left to right)\n        * Y-axis: Ranks `1` to `8` (bottom to top)\n\n        ## Piece encoding format: `[Type][Color][2Step][Moved]`\n        \n        * Type (Piece Type):\n            * `K` - King\n            * `Q`- Queen\n            * `R`- Rook\n            * `B` - Bishop\n            * `K` - Knight\n            * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as White.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |RBF0|NBF0|BBF0|QBF0|KBF0|BBF0|NBF0|RBF0| 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0| 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, P: e2-e4, P: e2-e3, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, N: g1-f3, N: g1-h3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': "Okay, let's see. I'm playing as White, and it's the start of the game. The board is set up in the initial position. All the pawns are on their starting ranks, and the pieces are behind them. The valid moves given are all the possible first moves for White.\n\nFirst, I need to recall the best opening moves. In chess, the most common first moves are moving the e-pawn or d-pawn forward two squares. That's because controlling the center is crucial. The moves e2-e4 and d2-d4 are the most popular. Looking at the valid moves list, e2-e4 and d2-d4 are options.\n\nLet me check the valid moves again. The list includes P: e2-e4, P: e2-e3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, etc. So e4 and d4 are there. Also, knight moves like N: b1-c3 and N: g1-f3 are options.\n\nWhy are e4 and d4 good? They open lines for the queen and bishops, and control the central squares (d4, d5, e4, e5). Starting with e4 is very aggressive and common. But in some openings, d4 is also strong. However, as White, moving e4 is often the top choice.\n\nOther pawn moves like a4 or h4 are less common because they don't contribute to center control and can weaken the position. The knight moves are okay but usually, developing knights after moving pawns is better.\n\nWait, the valid moves include P: e2-e4. Let me confirm. Yes, e2-e4 is listed. So that's a strong move. Also, d2-d4 is there. But e4 is generally considered the best first move.\n\nI should also consider if there's any immediate threat, but in the starting position, there's none. So the goal is to develop pieces and control the center.\n\nAnother thing to think about is whether to move a pawn two squares or one. e2-e4 is two squares, which is standard. e2-e3 is one square, which is less common because it doesn't challenge the center as much.\n\nSo between e4 and d4, e4 is probably better here. Also, the knights: moving N to f3 or c3 supports the center. But as the first move, pawn to e4 is ideal.\n\nWait, the user's valid moves list shows P: e2-e4 as an option. So choosing that would be the standard move. Let me check if there's any reason not to. In the initial position, all pawns are on second rank, so e2-e4 is allowed (since it's the first move for that pawn, can move two squares).\n\nYes, e2-e4 is a great move. It opens the diagonal for the bishop and queen, controls the d5 and f5 squares, and is the most common first move.\n\nOther options like d4 are also good, but e4 is more aggressive and leads to open games. Since the goal is to maximize winning chances, e4 is the way to go.\n\nSo the answer should be P: e2-e4.", 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the starting position with all pieces on their initial squares. As White, I have the first move advantage and must choose an opening that establishes central control and facilitates rapid development. The board is symmetric, with no immediate threats, so the focus is on optimal opening principles.\n\nEvaluation: Key considerations are center control (e4/d4 squares), piece development, and king safety. Top candidate moves:\n- **P: e2-e4**: Immediately claims central space, opens diagonals for the queen and light-squared bishop, and aligns with aggressive openings like the Italian Game or Ruy Lopez. Statistically the most successful first move for White.\n- **P: d2-d4**: Solid alternative controlling the center, leading to closed/semi-closed structures (e.g., Queen’s Gambit), but slightly less dynamic than e4 in maximizing winning chances.\n- **N: g1-f3**: Develops a knight while preparing kingside castling, but delays central pawn commitment. Less optimal as a first move compared to e4 since it doesn’t directly contest the center.\n\nReason: e2-e4 is the strongest theoretical and practical choice. It maximizes central influence, accelerates development, and creates immediate tension—critical for seizing initiative. While d4 is viable, e4 historically yields higher win rates for White and avoids passive structures. Knight moves waste tempo compared to central pawn thrusts.\n\nAnswer: P: e2-e4'}
