\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|    |RWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, B: c1-b2, B: c1-a3, P: c2-c3, B: c4-b3, B: c4-d3, B: c4-e2, B: c4-b5, B: c4-a6, B: c4-d5, Q: d1-e1, Q: d1-e2, Q: d1-f3, Q: d1-g4, Q: d1-h5, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, P: e6-e7, R: f1-e1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': "Okay, let's see. I'm playing as White, and I need to figure out the best move here. Let me start by understanding the current board setup.\n\nFirst, looking at the board representation provided. The rows are numbered from 8 at the top (Black's side) down to 1 at the bottom (White's side). Files are a to h left to right. So White's pieces are on ranks 1 and 2, Black's on 7 and 8, but there are some pieces in between.\n\nLet me parse the board:\n\nRow 8 (Black's back rank):\na8: empty\nb8: RBF1 (Black Rook, has moved)\nc8: empty\nd8: empty\ne8: KBF0 (Black King, hasn't moved, so maybe castling possible?)\nf8: BBF0 (Black Bishop, hasn't moved)\ng8: NBF0 (Black Knight, hasn't moved)\nh8: RBF0 (Black Rook, hasn't moved)\n\nRow 7:\na7: empty\nb7: empty\nc7: PBF0 (Black Pawn, hasn't moved)\nd7: empty\ne7: empty\nf7: empty\ng7: empty\nh7: empty\n\nRow 6:\na6: NBF1 (Black Knight, has moved)\nb6: empty\nc6: empty\nd6: PBF1 (Black Pawn, has moved)\ne6: PWF1 (White Pawn, has moved)\nf6: empty\ng6: empty\nh6: PBF1 (Black Pawn, has moved)\n\nRow 5:\na5: empty\nb5: empty\nc5: empty\nd5: PBF1 (Black Pawn)\ne5: empty\nf5: empty\ng5: PBF1 (Black Pawn)\nh5: empty\n\nRow 4:\na4: empty\nb4: PBF1 (Black Pawn)\nc4: BWF1 (White Bishop, has moved)\nd4: empty\ne4: empty\nf4: empty\ng4: empty\nh4: empty\n\nRow 3: all empty.\n\nRow 2 (White's pawns):\na2: PWF0 (White Pawn, hasn't moved)\nb2: empty\nc2: PWF0\nd2: PWF0\ne2: empty\nf2: PWF0\ng2: PWF0\nh2: PWF0\n\nRow 1 (White's back rank):\na1: RWF0 (White Rook, hasn't moved)\nb1: NWF0 (White Knight, hasn't moved)\nc1: BWF0 (White Bishop, hasn't moved)\nd1: QWF0 (White Queen, hasn't moved)\ne1: empty\nf1: RWF1 (White Rook, has moved)\ng1: KWF1 (White King, has moved)\nh1: empty\n\nWait, looking at row 1: e1 is empty? But the king is at g1 (KWF1), which is the White King. So White's king has moved (since Moved flag is 1). So castling kingside might not be possible because the king has already moved. Wait, the king's Moved flag is 1, so yes, he's moved before, so castling is no longer an option. Similarly, the rook on f1 is RWF1 (has moved), but the rook on a1 is RWF0 (hasn't moved). But since the king has moved, castling is invalid regardless.\n\nNow, the valid moves for White are listed. Let's look at them.\n\nPossible moves:\n\nP: a2-a4, P: a2-a3,\n\nN: b1-a3, N: b1-c3,\n\nB: c1-b2, B: c1-a3,\n\nP: c2-c3,\n\nB: c4-b3, B: c4-d3, B: c4-e2, B: c4-b5, B: c4-a6, B: c4-d5,\n\nQ: d1-e1, Q: d1-e2, Q: d1-f3, Q: d1-g4, Q: d1-h5,\n\nP: d2-d4, P: d2-d3,\n\nP: e6-e7,\n\nR: f1-e1,\n\nP: f2-f4, P: f2-f3,\n\nK: g1-h1,\n\nP: g2-g4, P: g2-g3,\n\nP: h2-h4, P: h2-h3.\n\nHmm. Let's analyze the position.\n\nFirst, White's position. The king is on g1 (has moved), so no castling. The rook on f1 has moved, but a1 rook hasn't. But again, king moved, so castling not possible.\n\nWhite's pawns: a2, c2, d2, f2, g2, h2 are all on their starting squares (PWF0), except e6 is a white pawn that has moved (PWF1). Wait, e6 is a white pawn? Wait row 6, e6 is PWF1. That's a white pawn on e6. How did it get there? Because pawns move forward, so for white, forward is up the board (from rank 2 to 8). Wait, no: White's pawns start on rank 2, move towards rank 8. So a white pawn on e6 would have moved from e2 to e3, e4, e5, e6. So it's on e6, which is rank 6, which is four squares up from rank 2. So it's a white pawn that's advanced quite far. But looking at the valid moves, there's a P: e6-e7. So that pawn can move to e7.\n\nWait, but why is there a white pawn on e6? Let's check the board again.\n\nRow 6: e6 is PWF1 (White Pawn, has moved). So yes, that pawn started on e2, moved to e3, e4, e5, e6. So it's on e6. And it can move to e7 next.\n\nNow, looking at Black's position. The king is on e8 (KBF0), which is Black's king, hasn't moved. So Black can potentially castle, but we need to check if the rooks have moved. On row 8, a8 is empty, b8 is RBF1 (rook has moved), h8 is RBF0 (rook hasn't moved). So Black's kingside rook (h8) hasn't moved, king hasn't moved, but are there pieces in between? On row 8, between e8 and h8: f8 is BBF0 (bishop), g8 is NBF0 (knight). So to castle kingside, Black would need to move king to g8 and rook to f8, but there are pieces in the way (f8 and g8 occupied), so castling kingside isn't possible. Queenside castling: king to c8, rook to d8. But between e8 and a8: d8 is empty, c8 empty, b8 is RBF1. Wait, row 8: a8 empty, b8 Rook, c8 empty, d8 empty, e8 king. So queenside castling would require moving king to c8 and rook from a8 to d8, but a8 is empty. Wait, h8 is RBF0 (rook hasn't moved), but a8 is empty. Wait, original setup for Black's back rank is R, N, B, Q, K, B, N, R. So on row 8, a8 should be rook, but in the current state, a8 is empty, b8 is rook. So maybe the a8 rook has been captured or moved. Wait, b8 is RBF1 (rook has moved), so perhaps the a8 rook is gone, and the b8 rook is actually the queenside rook that moved. Hmm, maybe not important right now.\n\nBut Black's king is on e8, hasn't moved. However, given the pieces around, castling might not be possible for Black either.\n\nNow, assessing threats. Let's see if White is in check. The king is on g1. Are any B