So that doesn\'t capture the pawn on b2.\n\nWhat about the bishop on d5? Can it attack b2? The bishop moves diagonally. From d5 to b2: d5 is file d, rank 5. b2 is file b, rank 2. The difference is 2 files left, 3 ranks down. That\'s not a diagonal (which would require same number of files and ranks). So no, the bishop can\'t reach b2.\n\nThe rook on a1: moving to b1 would put it on b1, attacking the pawn on b2. But as mentioned before, Black could then capture the rook with b2xa1=Q.\n\nWait, but if White plays R a1-b1, then Black\'s next move could be b2xa1=Q, which would capture the rook and promote. So White loses the rook and Black gets a queen. That\'s probably bad.\n\nAlternatively, can White play a move that blocks the pawn\'s advance? The pawn on b2 can move to b1. To block, White would need to occupy b1. So moving a piece to b1.\n\nPossible moves to b1:\n\n- Rook a1 to b1 (R a1-b1)\n\n- Knight c3 to b1 (N c3-b1)\n\nIf White moves a piece to b1, then the Black pawn can\'t advance to b1 (since it\'s occupied), but it could capture diagonally. Wait, Black pawn on b2 moves downward (toward rank 1). Its forward direction is down, so to promote it would go to b1. If b1 is occupied by a White piece, the pawn can\'t move there. However, pawns capture diagonally, so if there\'s a piece on a1 or c1, it could capture those. But if b1 is blocked, the pawn is stuck unless it can capture.\n\nSo if White places a piece on b1, the Black pawn on b2 is blocked. Then, on Black\'s turn, they can\'t move the pawn forward, but they might have other moves. However, Black may have other pieces to move, but the main threat is the promotion.\n\nSo the idea is to block the pawn\'s promotion square (b1) with a White piece.\n\nLooking at the valid moves:\n\nR a1-b1 would move the rook to b1, blocking promotion.\n\nN c3-b1 would move the knight to b1.\n\nWhich is better?\n\nIf rook moves to b1, then Black can capture it with b2xa1 (but a1 is where the rook came from; wait, no: rook is now on b1. Black pawn on b2 can capture on a1 or c1. So if rook is on b1, the pawn can\'t capture it directly. Wait, the pawn on b2, to capture, needs to go to a1 or c1. So if rook is on b1, the pawn\'s forward move is blocked, but it can\'t capture the rook because it\'s straight ahead, not diagonal. So the pawn would be blocked.\n\nWait, pawns move forward one (or two initially), but capture diagonally. So a pawn on b2 (Black) wants to go to b1. If b1 is occupied, it can\'t move there. It can only capture on a1 or c1. So if White blocks b1, the pawn is stuck.\n\nTherefore, moving a piece to b1 would prevent the promotion.\n\nSo options are R a1-b1 or N c3-b1.\n\nWhich is better?\n\nR a1-b1: uses the rook, which is a more valuable piece. If Black then has no other moves, it might lead to stalemate, but probably not. However, if White blocks with the rook, Black may have to move another piece, but the pawn is blocked. However, if Black can capture the rook on b1 somehow, but how?\n\nBlack\'s pieces: the pawn on b2 can\'t capture the rook on b1 (since it\'s straight ahead). The only way Black can capture the rook on b1 is if another piece attacks it. Looking at Black\'s pieces:\n\n- Rook on b4: attacks along rank 4 and file b. So on file b, from b4 down to b1. So if White\'s rook is on b1, Black\'s rook on b4 can capture it (R b4xb1). Oh! That\'s a problem.\n\nYes, because the Black rook is on b4, same file as b1. So if White moves rook to b1, Black can play R b4xb1, capturing the rook. So that would be bad for White: they block the pawn, but lose a rook.\n\nWhereas if White uses the knight to block on b1 (N c3-b1), then Black\'s rook on b4 can capture the knight (R b4xb1), but a knight is less valuable than a rook. However, White might prefer to lose a knight than a rook.\n\nBut wait, let\'s see:\n\nIf White plays N c3-b1:\n\n- Knight moves to b1, blocking promotion.\n\n- Black\'s turn: they can capture the knight with R b4xb1. Then the Black rook is on b1, and the pawn on b2 is still there. Then, on Black\'s next move, they could play b2-b1=Q (but rook is already there?), wait no: after R b4xb1, the rook is on b1, so pawn on b2 can\'t move to b1. Wait, but the pawn could capture the rook on b1 if it\'s diagonal. Wait, pawn captures diagonally. So if rook is on b1, pawn on b2 can capture it by moving to a1 or c1. But rook is on b1, which is straight ahead, so pawn can\'t capture it. So after R b4xb1, the pawn on b2 is still blocked by the rook on b1. Then Black would have to move something else, but the pawn is still stuck.\n\nWait, but Black\'s rook capturing on b1 would place the rook on b1, blocking its own pawn. So the pawn can\'t promote. But then Black is the one who blocked their own pawn. However, Black could then move the rook away on their next turn, allowing the pawn to promote. Hmm, this is getting complicated.\n\nAlternatively, if White blocks with the knight on b1, and Black captures it with the rook, then the pawn is still blocked by the rook. But Black can then on their next move move the rook away, allowing the pawn to promote. So maybe it\'s not a good idea.\n\nWait, let\'s think step by step.\n\nOption 1: White plays N c3-b1.\n\n- Blocks b1.\n\n- Black\'s move options:\n\n   - Capture knight with R b4xb1. Now rook is on b1.\n\n   - Then, pawn on b2 is blocked by rook on b1. Black\'s next move could be to move the rook away (e.g., R b1-b2, but that\'s forward for Black rook? Wait, rook can move any direction. So Black could move rook from b1 to, say, b8, then pawn promotes. But after R b4xb1, it\'s Black\'s move. They could choose to not move the rook, but then pawn is still blocked. However, Black might have better moves than capturing the knight.\n\nAlternatively, Black might ignore the knight and promote: b2-b1=Q. But if b1 is occupied by the knight, they can\'t. So Black must deal with the knight.\n\nSo Black\'s best move would be to capture the knight with the rook, leading to rook on b1, blocking the pawn. Then White\'s next move could be to do something else, but the pawn is still blocked. However, Black can then on their next turn move the rook away and promote.\n\nBut perhaps White can then capture the pawn. Wait, after R b4xb1, the pawn is on b2, blocked by rook on b1. If Black moves rook away, say R b1-a1, then pawn promotes to b1. But White might have a chance to capture.\n\nThis is getting too speculative. Maybe a better approach is to capture the pawn immediately if possible.\n\nWait, is there any way to capture the b2 pawn right now?\n\nLooking at White\'s pieces:\n\n- The king can\'t reach it.\n\n- The rook on a1 could capture if it were on b1, but to get there, it\'s R a1-b1, which doesn\'t capture anything (b1 is empty).\n\n- The knight on c3: can it capture on b2? The move from c3 to b2 is not a valid knight move (needs to be L-shape). c3 to b2 is one left, one down, which is a king\'s move, not knight\'s. So no.\n\n- Bishop on d5: can it reach b2? d5 to b2 is two left, three down. Not diagonal, so no.\n\n- Pawns: White\'s pawns on a2, c2, d2, etc. The pawn on c2 (PWF0) could capture on b3 or d3, but b2 is diagonal forward for Black pawn, not for White. White pawns move up, so from c2, White pawn captures on b3 or d3. Not relevant for b2.\n\nSo no White piece can capture the b2 pawn immediately.\n\nThus, the best option is to block the promotion square b1.\n\nBetween R a1-b1 and N c3-b1.\n\nAs mentioned earlier, R a1-b1 risks losing the rook to R b4xb1.\n\nN c3-b1 risks losing the knight to the same.\n\nBut a knight is less valuable than a rook, so maybe better to use the knight.\n\nHowever, after N c3-b1, Black plays R b4xb1, then White might have other threats.\n\nAlternatively, is there another way to prevent promotion?\n\nWhat if White advances a pawn to attack the b2 pawn\'s promotion square?\n\nWait, the pawn on a2: if White plays a2-a4 or a3, not sure.\n\nOr, the pawn on c2: but c2 is White\'s pawn (PWF0), but in the board state, row 2\'s c2 is P