 Not helpful.\n\nB d5-e6: e6 is empty.\n\nNot sure if attacking the king is feasible now.\n\nBack to the main threat: b2 pawn promoting.\n\nIf White doesn\'t block b1, Black will promote next move. So White must make a move that either blocks b1 or captures the pawn (but can\'t capture now).\n\nSo between R a1-b1 and N c3-b1.\n\nLet\'s evaluate both:\n\n1. R a1-b1:\n\n   - Blocks b1.\n\n   - Black can respond with R b4xb1, capturing the rook. Now Black has a rook on b1, blocking their own pawn.\n\n   - Then, Black\'s pawn on b2 is still blocked. Black might move the rook away next turn to promote.\n\n   - White loses a rook for a knight (since Black had to use their rook to capture, but White\'s rook is more valuable).\n\n   - Not great.\n\n2. N c3-b1:\n\n   - Blocks b1 with knight.\n\n   - Black can capture with R b4xb1.\n\n   - White loses a knight, but saves the rook.\n\n   - Then, Black has rook on b1 blocking pawn.\n\n   - Black can move rook away next turn to promote.\n\n   - But knight is less valuable, so this is better for White.\n\nHowever, is there a way to prevent Black from capturing the blocking piece?\n\nAlternatively, what if White moves the king towards the pawn?\n\nK h1-g1. This move is listed as valid.\n\nAfter K g1, what happens?\n\nBlack\'s next move: b2-b1=Q, promoting to queen. Then White is in big trouble, as Black has a new queen.\n\nSo that\'s bad. So king moving away doesn\'t help.\n\nAnother idea: can White use the pawn on a2 to capture on b3 or something? Not directly relevant.\n\nWait, pawn on a2 can move to a3 or a4. If a2-a3, then maybe support something, but not sure.\n\nAlternatively, the rook on f2 can move to f1, which is near the king. But not sure.\n\nWait, another thought: the Black pawn on b2 is on a light square (b2 is a dark square? Wait, chess boards alternate colors. a1 is dark, so b2 is dark. Bishop on d5: d5 is a light square (a1 dark, a2 light, ..., d5: d is file 4, 1+4+5=10 even? Maybe better not to get bogged down. The bishop on d5 is White\'s bishop; assuming it\'s on a light square, it can only move on light squares. The pawn on b2 is on b2, which is a dark square. So the bishop can\'t attack it.\n\nHmm.\n\nWait, what about the knight on c3 moving to e4 (N c3-e4). What does that do? Maybe threatens something. Or N c3-d1, which is towards the king side.\n\nAlternatively, the bishop on d5 moving to c4 (B d5-c4). What\'s on c4? Empty. Could that be preparing to pin something?\n\nNot sure.\n\nBack to the main issue: must prevent b2 promotion.\n\nAnother angle: if White can check the Black king, forcing them to respond, thereby preventing the promotion.\n\nIs there a check available?\n\nWhite\'s pieces:\n\n- Bishop on d5: can it check the king? Black king is on h8.\n\nBishop moves: from d5, possible checks would be on the same diagonal. h8 is on the a1-h8 diagonal, but d5 is not on that diagonal. The diagonal from d5 going up-right would be e6, f7, g8. g8 is empty, so B d5-g8 doesn\'t check king on h8.\n\nKnight on c3: can it check h8? No, too far.\n\nRooks: f2 rook moving to f8 would check, but f8 is empty. R f2-f8 is a valid move. Let\'s see:\n\nR f2-f8. Then rook checks Black king on h8? No, because f8 and h8 are on the same rank. Yes! If rook moves to f8, it\'s on the same rank as h8 (rank 8), so it would give check.\n\nWait, row 8 has h8 as king, others empty. So R f2-f8 would place rook on f8, attacking along rank 8, thus checking the Black king.\n\nThat\'s a valid move (R: f2-f8 is in the list).\n\nIf White plays R f2-f8+, check.\n\nThen Black must respond to the check. Possible responses:\n\n- Move king to g8 or h7.\n\n- Block with a piece (but Black has few pieces; on rank 8, only king, so no blockers).\n\nSo Black would have to move the king.\n\nAfter that, Black\'s pawn on b2 still threatens to promote, but they had to use their move to deal with the check.\n\nThis could be a way to delay the promotion.\n\nLet\'s see:\n\nWhite plays R f2-f8+, check.\n\nBlack must move ki